This is a very big upgrade as this is where we will see both the code work simultaneoulsy complementing each other at the basic level. The grid is formed intially with randomly distributed cells. I first made two independent codes- one for the discrete heat diffusion equation solver, another for CA analysis that has been made until now. These two were later combined in a singular code and then tweaked accordingly to reflect the true physics. 
The heat source inputs heat into the grid. The thermal solver will solve the heat diffusion equation and then calculate the thermal gradients and the cooling rate formed on the basis of the heat source motion.

-------------------------------------------------------------

The initial changes were
-to have a smaller grain size (more number of nuclie : increased from 20  to 1600)
-to have a gaussian heat source that moves on a straight line with a specified heat input radius and decay area
-incorporate the aspects of cooling_coefficient that represents the convective losses indirectly, density, thermal diffusivity and cp of the material to model a sensible heat input, effects of ambient temperatures, constant wall temperatures which act heat sinks and other physical properties. 

The flow of the code is as the first part forms a random distribution of grains in our observation window. The heat source is then given to the sample which raises the temperature and causes the melting to begin. Meanwhile the motion of heat source is also carried out. In the same timestep, the heat diffusion solver calculates the new temperature gradients and updates the thermal profile for the next timestep to solve for grain growth. The CA part then governs the grain growth based on the current thermal situation which is updated in every timestep. 


The current model completely lacks the inclusion of thermal stresses and fluid motion in the molten weld pool, as well as dimensional relevance to reality. Appropriate scaling was done by hit and trial and with references of guides. 
